namespace Eling.Backend.Mcp;

/// <summary>
/// Static text delivered to MCP clients during the initialize handshake.
/// </summary>
public static class ServerInstructions
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Individual instruction sections broken down by semantic responsibility.
    /// </summary>
    public static readonly string[] Sections =
    [
        "Eling is a durable markdown-backed memory system. Memory Markdown files under '.eling/memories/' are the canonical source of truth and MUST be tracked in Git.",

        "Generated runtime files MUST be added to '.gitignore': logs, the runtime directory '.eling/runtime/', and every SQLite database plus its sidecars. Sidecars are always '<name>.db-<suffix>' — '-journal', '-wal', '-shm', '-wal2' — so prefer the patterns '*.db' and '*.db-*' over naming each suffix; that stays complete as SQLite adds more and does not over-match unrelated names like '*.dbf'. When running in a project workspace, always check that '.gitignore' ignores these runtime files while keeping '.eling/memories/' tracked; prompt the user for confirmation to fix '.gitignore' if needed.",

        "'Eling <something>' (Javanese for 'remember <something>') is a recall instruction: retrieve the memory previously stored in Eling (e.g. 'eling build steps' recalls the build steps saved in memories).",

        "All memory operations MUST use Eling MCP tools — never read, search, or write '.eling/memories/' files directly.",

        "Memory Save & Scope Rules: Default to scope=project-local and always pass scope explicitly — the backend default is project-local as well, but never rely on it. Decision order: knowledge that is true and useful in a DIFFERENT, unrelated project (general coding knowledge, cross-project preferences, tooling and workflow rules, lessons about your own behavior) goes to scope=global. Anything bound to THIS repo — decisions, conventions, repo-specific lessons, in-progress or scratch state, machine-specific notes — goes to scope=project-local: machine-only, never committed, shared across worktrees of one repo. Use scope=project (committed .eling/memories/, shared via git) ONLY when the user explicitly wants a specific memory shared for this repo — 'probably shareable someday' is not a signal, and the user's standing rule is that project-local outranks project in every tie. Never ask the user which scope to pick; decide with this order. An explicit scope=project save needs this workspace's own .eling scope: in an ancestor-scope workspace without one it returns init-required — offer memory_init_project then; in an empty chain (no .eling anywhere up) the save is not blocked but lands in project-local with a note — offer memory_init_project only when the user wants that memory shared via git. Default and explicit project-local saves never require initialization. Write memory content in English by default (or the user's preferred native language if requested), but ALWAYS use lowercase English tags for consistent indexing.",

        "Consistency Rules: Use clean lowercase English memory tags (no underscores, single words/concise phrases). Keep memories portable — avoid machine-specific absolute paths and personal usernames; use relative paths or generic placeholders so memories stay consistent across machines and platforms.",

        "Zero-Agents & Autonomous Guidance (HARD RULE): Eling operates under a Zero-Agents philosophy. Users do NOT need to configure 'AGENTS.md'. Eling educates and directs agents autonomously via MCP server instructions, tool schemas, and operational cues. The agent MUST proactively adhere to Eling routines (session-start recall, transition recall, tier-based saving) without waiting for user reminders or workspace configuration files.",

        "Memory Recall Strategy: Trigger `memory_recall` on clear phase transitions — (1) On every topic shift or progression to the next plan step/subtask, (2) Before starting a new task/spec implementation to check project rules, (3) Before preparing git commits to verify repo hygiene, (4) When debugging recurring errors/failures, or (5) On explicit user recall ('eling <topic>'). DO NOT recall on greeting/casual chat or repeated identical micro-steps in the same step to preserve context window and latency.",

        "Mandatory Session-Start Recall (HARD RULE): Before any response or tool call in the first user turn of every new session, invoke `memory_recall` with a BROAD set of topics — ones derived from the opening message AND ones covering other areas: standing rules and preferences, standing decisions, lessons and known gotchas, workflow and repo conventions, and recently touched work. Never anchor session-start recall solely on the opening message's subject. Use the exact call shape `{\"context\": {\"topics\": [\"...\", \"...\"]}}` — `topics` nested under `context` (a top-level `topics` alias is also accepted). After the call, check `stats.recallCount`: if it is 0, the call was mis-shaped or nothing matched — re-issue with the nested shape and broader terms (bounded retries; never an unbounded loop) before treating recall as done. Every recall also returns `standingMemories` — a topic-independent baseline of standing rules/preferences, standing decisions, and lessons (project-first, capped); read it on session start even when your topic call was narrow, because it is the server-enforced consistency spine. Do this BEFORE inspecting files, or writing any reply — even when the opening message looks casual or ambiguous. This makes recall the default behavior; users do not need to configure `AGENTS.md` globally.",

        "Transition & Step Progression Recall (HARD RULE): Whenever moving to a new step in an implementation plan, switching tasks, or when the user introduces a new topic, invoke `memory_recall` with topics for the new step BEFORE executing actions. This eliminates assumption drift across steps and keeps workflow routines consistent.",

        "Recall Overflow Summary: when topic recall returns more relevant items than `recallLimit`, the response carries `recallSummary` — digest paragraphs covering the hits the limit would otherwise drop (never silently skipped). `source` is 'judge' when the semantic summarizer wrote them and 'heuristic' when it degraded to a deterministic digest, with the failure cause in `note`; pass `summarizeOverflow: false` to skip the summary for minimum latency. Read it instead of assuming nothing beyond the returned list matched.",

        "Mandatory Save (HARD RULE): Whenever the turn contains something meant to outlive this conversation, call `memory_save` BEFORE replying. Tier 1 — what the user states: a standing rule or preference ('always', 'never', 'from now on', 'prefer X', 'stop doing Y'), a correction of a fact, convention, or past mistake, a decision and its rationale, or an explicit 'remember this' / 'ingat ya'. Tier 2 — what you observe while working: a project or environment fact that is NOT derivable from the tree (a gotcha, a platform/port/tooling quirk, a wrong assumption the evidence just disproved, where something actually lives, which of two competing ways is the real one). Do not wait for the words 'remember' or 'ingat' when Tier 1 is present; infer the trigger. An acknowledgement is never a substitute: replying 'okay', 'noted', 'siap', 'sure' WITHOUT a `memory_save` call is a failure, not a save — acknowledge and save, the two are additive. SKIP transient task state, speculation, and anything unverified. SKIP anything the repository already records (AGENTS.md, code, specs, README, git history) — the tree remembers those, and a memory that restates them is noise. SKIP a near-duplicate of a memory `memory_recall` already surfaced this turn. Map the tier to `type`: Tier 1 rules and preferences → `preference`, corrections → `lesson`, decisions → `decision`, Tier 2 observations → `fact`. Record how far the observation was verified (which file, which run, which platform) so a later agent knows where it stops holding.",

        "Memory Content Shape (HARD RULE): A memory's content must BE the decision, rule, lesson, or fact — standalone durable guidance a future session can apply without knowing the conversation that produced it. Never write the exchange itself: no narration ('the user said X, then I...'), no diary framing ('earlier today', 'in this session'), no advice that only makes sense inside the current chat, and no quoting the user. Keep what must survive — the decision, its rationale, its scope, and how far it was verified (which file, which run, which platform) — then stop.",

        "Project Scope Initialization: initializing `.eling` — the committed, git-shared tier — requires explicit user consent; `project-local` and `global` saves never need it. Check project posture via `projectScope.adoptable` in recall results (or `memory_project_status`) before an explicit scope=project save: `initialized: false` describes only the committed tier, never memory availability. If `adoptable: true`, PROACTIVELY ask the user for consent before calling `memory_init_project`. If the user declines (policy `disabled`), route project-bound saves to scope=project-local (machine-only, never committed) without prompting again.",

        "Tool responses carry provenance: `projectName`/`projectRoot` identify the scope level a memory lives in (own, an ancestor, or null for global); `memory_get`/`list`/`search` return scoped payloads.",

        "File Operation Preference: For file operations inside the workspace, PREFER Eling's sandboxed file tools over host-native equivalents (`file_read`, `file_search`, `glob`, `directory_list`, `path_test`, `file_edit`, `file_write`, `file_append`, `file_move`, `file_copy`, `file_delete`, `directory_create`, `directory_delete`). Resolve paths relative to the workspace root reported by `workspace_root`. These tools are audited via telemetry and enforce the sandbox; pass `lineNumbers=true` to `file_read` for line-numbered output. This is a preference, not a mandate — host-native tools and read-only `allowExternal=true` remain acceptable fallbacks, especially when the operation is outside the project root or the host tool is materially easier in context. IMPORTANT: `file_write` is preferred for creating NEW files; overwriting an existing file with it is allowed only when truly necessary, otherwise modify existing files surgically with `file_edit`.",

        "Codebase Indexing (HARD RULE): Eling maintains a per-workspace FTS index of the working directory — the folder the backend was launched in, never the nearest `.eling` ancestor — (porter + trigram, BM25-ranked) so agents can search code without opening a terminal. Tools: `codebase_index` (build/refresh; incremental by default, `full:true` rebuilds, `paths` scopes to subfolders), `codebase_search` (query plus line ranges plus snippets ready to inject; `scope:'project'` default (this workspace) or `'all'` for every alive workspace, `projects:[...]` for explicit roots, `pathPrefix`/`filePattern` to narrow), `codebase_status` (file/chunk counts, last indexed time, watcher state, owning workspace root). In the first user turn of every new session, inspect the `codebase` field already attached to the `memory_recall` response (recall carries it whenever the indexer is available — do NOT call `codebase_status` separately in that case; only fall back to `codebase_status` if the `codebase` field is absent) BEFORE any code search or file inspection. If no index exists (fileCount 0), ASK the user for approval to run `codebase_index` and wait for the answer; never start indexing without consent. If the index is stale (lastIndexedAt is not today, or pendingFiles > 0), also ask before refreshing.",

        "Codebase Search (HARD RULE): When searching code, symbols, implementations, or text across the workspace, ALWAYS call `codebase_search` first. DO NOT use native `grep`, `file_search`, or shell commands as the primary lookup tool — bypassing `codebase_search` when an index exists is a DIRECT RULE VIOLATION. `codebase_search` provides ranked BM25 + trigram snippets with line ranges that save tokens and search latency. Native `grep` is strictly a secondary fallback, permitted ONLY when: (1) `codebase_search` explicitly returns 0 hits, (2) the query requires complex regex unsupported by SQLite FTS, or (3) codebase index creation was denied by the user.",

        "Codebase Index Notes: The index is a rebuildable cache (deleting the DB loses nothing — just re-run `codebase_index`); a debounced background watcher keeps it fresh on the dashboard owner, so after edits made outside the agent's own writes, allow a moment for the batch to drain or re-run `codebase_index`, and check the `codebase` field's freshness (from the `memory_recall` response, or `codebase_status` only if that field is absent). `.gitignore` rules are honored on top of the built-in excluded dirs. `codebase_search` never throws on a missing index — it returns empty hits with a hint to index first.",


        "Database connections: five read-only tools — db_list_connections, db_get_schema, db_query, db_detect_connections, db_import_connections. db_query only runs SELECT/EXPLAIN/PRAGMA; mutations are rejected. db_detect_connections previews connection strings from the backend appsettings.json with passwords masked; db_import_connections registers only the keys the user explicitly approves, as read-only connections. Never import without explicit approval. db_list_connections now returns BOTH the registered connections (Registered) and any connections found in appsettings.json that are not yet registered (Unimported) — so unimported connections are never missed just because the agent only listed. When Unimported is non-empty, OFFER THE USER OPTIONS (via the question tool — e.g. 'Import all', 'Import selected', 'Skip') and only call db_import_connections with the explicitly approved keys. Never auto-import. Do not run db_detect_connections on every chat start — db_list_connections already surfaces unimported ones on demand."    ];

    /// <summary>
    /// Merged instruction text joined by double newlines for clear paragraph separation.
    /// </summary>
    public static readonly string Default = string.Join("\n\n", Sections);
}

